  "interpretation": "## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 4,491 people took 2,394,107 actions, and 56.58% came back on more than one day. Weekly use dipped from 512 people in the week of 2026-08-30 to 323 in the week of 2026-09-20. It then rose to 1,447 people and 452,975 actions in the week of 2026-11-15.\n\nIn the latest 14 days, people rose 16.99% and actions rose 10.15%. People grew faster than actions. This may suggest newcomers start with lighter work. Upgrades moved quickly: release 2.6.12 reached 39.78% of weekly users, then 2.6.13 reached 46.95% in a partial week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nThe top actions are writing cell values (432,876), reading cell values (412,431) and writing formulas (198,090). Cells and tables make up 64.51% of actions. \"Share of work\" is an estimate using fixed effort levels: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3 and heavy data work counts 10. It is not time or speed. Macros are 6.71% of actions but 13.45% of estimated work, and Power Query data imports are 2.99% versus 8.25%.\n\nSince 2026-09-28, command line users averaged 1,562.0 actions each, against 295.4 for AI assistant users. These are different people over a shorter window, so treat this with care. Command line use leans on cells (73.69% of actions); assistant users use macros more (8.91% versus 0.76%).\n\nIn 30 days of assistant sessions, a typical session had 14.0 actions, and 73.14% touched more than one area, most often cells with worksheets. Sessions of 201+ actions were 5.08% of sessions but 48.99% of actions. Of 3,975 new people, 48.23% returned after a week and 34.29% after three weeks. Workdays averaged 55,230 actions versus 26,013 on weekend days. Many try advanced areas early: 81.8% of Power Query users did so on day one. Typical waits were 1.61 seconds for Power Query and 0.16 for cells. Waits depend on workbook size and computer, so they are not a quality score.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nDownloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, updates and automated installs add to totals, and channels overlap, so they should not be added up. GitHub release files lead with 34,500, then the npm assistant package with 21,523, VS Code installs with 10,569 and the npm command line with 9,079. npm totals cover the last 12 months; others count since each channel began.\n\nWeekly npm downloads peaked at 7,459 in the week of 2026-10-25 and fell to 2,157 by the week of 2026-11-15. Release 2.6.12 was downloaded most (1,760), then 2.6.11 (1,323). Weekly gains for GitHub release files ranged from 1,120 to 3,005 in full weeks, and VS Code from 231 to 338.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make writing values and formulas smoother, since they lead use. We will add guidance for macros and Power Query, which carry heavy estimated work. We will also help new people find value in their first weeks, to lift return rates.",
